DESCRIPTION:Join the GW Women's\, Gender\, and Sexuality Studies (WGSS) Pr
 ogram for an exciting talk from Visiting Assistant Professor Eva Pensis! A
 cross the late 20th century\, the popular music industry faced a series of
  scandals in live performance with the widespread use of lip-syncing. Foll
 owing the advent of recorded sound\, the commercial use of lip-sync in the
  popular music industry sought to divide the recorded performance from the
  performer with the aim of controlling both\, most often eliding the visua
 l presence of African American musicians while profiting from their sonic 
 contributions. Framed by the media theorist Moya Bailey's concept of "miso
 gynoir\," this presentation considers the case of Bl\nCategory: Academic\n
