DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261117T170000
DTSTAMP:20261105T195953Z
UID:a445b94fc61361bdf0d98f4b9e89@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Academic\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a book launch celebrating Professor of English Ale
 xa Alice Joubin's new book\, Transitional Shakespeare\, Gender\, and Adapt
 ion . About the Book The cross-gender role is a staple of Shakespeare’s 
 plays\, and drama is a transitional art. Gender is made flesh and brought 
 into being by actors’ embodiment. Similarly\, adaptations also operate t
 ransitionally. Some adaptations translate narratives into hospitable forms
  of expression. Other adaptations function as minor uses of established ge
 nres and gender categories. In Transitional Shakespeare\, Gender\, and Ada
 ption \, Professor Alexa Alice Joubin\, PhD\, theorizes “transness” in
  a number of modes\, including composite figures in drama that ar\nCategor
 y: Academic\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/book-launch-
 transitional-shakespeare-gender-and-adaption
LOCATION:Duques Hall\, 2201 G Street NW\, Washington DC 20052
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/book-launch-transitional-shakespeare-ge
 nder-and-adaption
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The State of American Democracy: Elections in the Digital Media Ag
 e — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261117T160000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261117T180000
DTSTAMP:20261105T195953Z
UID:404e1cc2d20a9d831d7ab98daa2a@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Lectures & Speakers\,Washington\, DC\,United St
 ates
DESCRIPTION:As local journalism declines\, media ownership consolidates\, 
 and Americans increasingly turn to social media and AI tools for political
  information\, the American information landscape is undergoing a profound
  transformation. What do these changes mean for public trust\, political p
 articipation and the health of our democracy? In the wake of the 2026 midt
 erm elections\, the Institute for Data\, Democracy and Politics (IDDP) wil
 l host a keynote by leading independent journalist Aaron Parnas \, and a p
 anel discussion featuring Michelle Cottle of the New York Times \, Letici
 a Bode of Georgetown University\, and Danny Hayes of George Washington Uni
 versity\, moderated by Michael Tomasky of the New Republi\nCategory: Lect
 ures & Speakers\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/the-stat
 e-of-american-democracy-elections-in-the-digital-media-age
LOCATION:Milken Institute School of Public Health Building\, 950 New Hamps
 hire Avenue NW\, Washington DC 20052
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/the-state-of-american-democracy-electio
 ns-in-the-digital-media-age
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
